    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Given:\n- Line 1: \\( y = mx + 4 \\)\n- Line 2: \\( y = 3x + b \\)\n- Intersection point: \\( (6, 10) \\)\n\nSince the lines intersect at \\( (6, 10) \\), their \\( y \\)-values must be equal when substituting \\( x = 6 \\).\n\nStep 1: Substitute \\( x = 6 \\) and \\( y = 10 \\) into the equations for both lines.\n\nFor Line 1:\n\\[\n10 = m \\cdot 6 + 4\n\\]\n\nFor Line 2:\n\\[\n10 = 3 \\cdot 6 + b\n\\]\n\nStep 2: Solve each equation for \\( m \\) and \\( b \\).\n\nFor Line 1:\n\\[\n10 = 6m + 4\n\\]\nSubtract 4 from both sides:\n\\[\n10 - 4 = 6m\n\\]\n\\[\n6 = 6m\n\\]\nDivide both sides by 6:\n\\[\nm = 1\n\\]\n\nFor Line 2:\n\\[\n10 = 18 + b\n\\]\nSubtract 18 from both sides:\n\\[\n10 - 18 = b\n\\]\n\\[\nb = -8\n\\]\n\nStep 3: Calculate \\( b + m \\).\n\n\\[\nb + m = -8 + 1 = -7\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-7}\n\\]",
